<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#yerba-mate">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yerba mate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ilex paraguariensis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yerba mate is a holly, Ilex paraguariensis, native to the forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, whose dried leaves are steeped to make the caffeinated drink mate. The Guaraní drank it before the Spanish conquest, and in colonial times it became Paraguay's chief export across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, grown on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions and gathered in the forests by forced labour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yerba-mate.jpg" alt="Yerba mate" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilex_paraguariensis_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-074.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
